rota lap 4515, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears; it asks for the page nobody answered, and the stash stack is this tree's answer file: 17 parked laps nobody had ever read back
think Opened on the round-open's own stash, made 90 seconds before this lap read the tree. Its base was 1cb5c85d4, one commit behind HEAD, so a wholesale restore would have reverted the peer commit c496ebba9.
think Read the parked log before touching a file. It carried status GREEN naming three witnesses, written before its send, so recovery could be a verification rather than a reconstruction from the diff.
think Recovered the seven files the peer commit never touched with git checkout stash@{0} -- <paths>, a TREE read, so mode 100755 survives where a blob read lands 100644.
think Applied the two shared pins -- ITINERARY.md and the day shelf -- as three-way patches instead, since both moved in c496ebba9. Both applied cleanly.
think Re-ran the three named witnesses at THIS head rather than trusting the parked claim: scope_trace 40 behaviors 0 faults, scope_rank GREEN, shell_dialect GREEN.
think Then asked the aether question of the whole stack rather than of my own stash: how many of 17 parked laps hold work no commit ever landed?
obs A first count -- files whose stash content differs from HEAD -- read 9 of 9 unlanded for my stash and 7 to 20 for the rest, and it was the wrong reading. A shared pin ALWAYS differs once HEAD moves, so that measure counts the pin, never the work.
obs The honest measure is files ADDED by the stash that no commit anywhere holds: git cat-file -e HEAD:$f over the A-status set. Total across 17 stashes: 9 files, and 2 of them are mine.
obs The other 7 are five fold archives -- ITINERARY landed-account shelves and REDS fold pages -- named by two stashes apiece. Their ROWS all stand: %470 in REDS-the-cheaper-question-an-instrument-asked-rows-470-471.md, %505 in REDS-a-duplicate-that-was-never-a-judgment-rows-592.md. A peer folded the same content under a different filename, so what died is the name rather than the record.
obs So a stack that reads like seventeen losses holds one, and that one was ninety seconds old. The round-open's stash is a safety net that has caught exactly one live thing in four days -- which is both the case for keeping it and the case for letting it forget.
obs Not taken: dropping the sixteen proved-redundant stashes. A stash drop is unrecoverable by ordinary means and the tree's own law puts destruction behind Keaton's word.
obs The ITINERARY block cost more attention than the recovery. The card stood at 40,667 of 40,960 and closed at 40,927 -- 33 bytes of slack, reached by five trimming passes.
loom stash_census stashes=17 files_added_total=9 lost_new_files=2 fold_archive_names_died=5 rows_recovered_elsewhere=2
loom scope_trace_control behaviors=40 faults=0
file tools/fixtures/s/scope_match.sh recovered -- set -f around the row split, so a glob reads as a pattern
